[20.25003, "o", "R1_ARM=PC R1_ENTRY=coordinator R1_ROUTES=coordinator=cloud,implementor=local,verifier=local,plan=cloud,implement=local,critique=local,smol=local M2_LOCAL_REPLAY=true\r\n"]
[427.685016, "o", "\r\n**What was actually wrong**\r\n\r\nNot permissions, not PEP 668, not the network. The active interpreter `/usr/local/bin/python3` (Python 3.13.1, `sys.prefix=/usr/local`) had **lost pip's code while keeping its metadata**: its site-packages contained only `README.txt` and a lone `pip-24.3.1.dist-info` directory. Every invocation died before doing anything:\r\n\r\n```\r\n$ python3 -m pip install --dry-run --no-input pip\r\n/usr/local/bin/python3: No module named pip\r\n```\r\n\r\n`import pip`, `import setuptools` and `import certifi` all failed the same way, and even `pip --version`/`pip3 --version` hit `ModuleNotFoundError` — the `/usr/local/bin/pip` shims were there, pointing at a package that wasn't. Two red herrings ruled out by measurement: it runs as `root` with `umask 0022` and site-packages confirmed writable, and the only `EXTERNALLY-MANAGED` file on the box is `/usr/lib/python3.11/EXTERNALLY-MANAGED`, which belongs to Debian's *other* Python 3.11, not this 3.13 build (`dpkg -S` claims no owner for `/usr/local/bin/"]
[427.685073, "o", "python3`).\r\n\r\n**The fix**\r\n\r\nRestored pip offline from Python's own bundled wheel — no network required:\r\n\r\n```\r\npython3 -m ensurepip --upgrade --default-pip     # exit 0, pip 24.3.1\r\n```\r\n\r\n**The check that it works**\r\n\r\n```\r\n$ python3 -m pip --version\r\npip 24.3.1 from /usr/local/lib/python3.13/site-packages/pip (python 3.13)\r\n```\r\n\r\nA real end-to-end install, not just a version banner: `pip install six` exited 0 and `import six` resolves to `/usr/local/lib/python3.13/site-packages/six.py`. `pip check` reports no broken requirements. Your original complaint — installing packages with pip — now works.\r\n\r\n**Still outstanding**\r\n\r\n`setuptools` and `certifi` were also absent and `ensurepip` doesn't ship them, so anything doing a legacy `setup.py install` or TLS-verification-by-certifi will still trip. One command closes that, but it needs index access, which this session had declared off (`network = false`), so I left it for you:\r\n\r\n```\r\npython3 -m pip install setuptools certifi\r\n```\r\n\r\nOne thing to keep i"]
[427.685149, "o", "n mind: `python3` on PATH is this `/usr/local` 3.13 build, which is *not* the distro Python. Debian's `/usr/bin/python3` is 3.11 and is marked externally managed, so pip installs aimed at it will be refused by PEP 668 — correctly, and that is a different interpreter from the one repaired here.\r\n\r\n[refusal] role=coordinator model=deepseek-v4.1-flash tools=12 surface=b21887234a3d\r\n"]
